r:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;to the 27th. The practical effect is that both giants begin away from home in matchday two.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The league itself has changed shape. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Girona</span><span>, the romance story of 2024, are down. Up come </span><span style="font-weight:700">Racing de Santander</span><span>&nbsp;as second-tier champions after fourteen years away, </span><span style="font-weight:700">RC Deportivo</span><span>, and </span><span style="font-weight:700">M&aacute;laga CF</span><span>&nbsp;through the play-offs. Racing marked the return by prising </span><span style="font-weight:700">Julen Agirrezabala</span><span>&nbsp;out of </span><span style="font-weight:700">Athletic Club</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;permanently &mdash; a deal unblocked in forty-eight hours by restructuring around a buy-back clause and a sell-on percentage after Athletic refused a loan and held out for around &euro;7m.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>The genuinely underreported Spanish story is the widening gap between the two economies inside one league. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sevilla</span><span>&nbsp;are operating on a salary limit of roughly &euro;22m &mdash; the lowest in the division &mdash; and sold </span><span style="font-weight:700">Akor Adams</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Venezia</span><span>&nbsp;for &euro;16.8m plus variables purely to create room, having already lost </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fede Vi&ntilde;as</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Toluca</span><span>&nbsp;for a figure roughly double their own valuation. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Valencia</span><span>&rsquo;s limit was raised to &euro;95.6m and they still lost </span><span style="font-weight:700">Thomas Meunier</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sunderland</span><span>&nbsp;because they could only offer a year plus an option against two guaranteed. At the other end, Barcelona have been restored to the 1:1 rule on the back of Lewandowski&rsquo;s wage coming off the books and a Spotify Camp Nou returning revenue above projection in what is the first full season back at the rebuilt stadium, with a target capacity of 104,600. Whether the VIP-seat income is being counted twice in that arithmetic is an argument currently running in the Catalan press, and it is a better story than most transfer rumours. Barcelona&rsquo;s Joan Gamper Trophy &mdash; against </span><span style="font-weight:700">Al Ahly</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">, on 19 August &mdash; will be the competition&rsquo;s first at the Camp Nou since the rebuild.</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Germany &middot; Bundesliga</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Germany does not kick a competitive first-division ball until </span><span style="font-weight:700">Friday 28 August</span><span>, and the week&rsquo;s biggest German story was decided not on a pitch but at the </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bundeskartellamt</span><span>. The Federal Cartel Office closed its long-running antitrust proceeding into the </span><span style="font-weight:700">50+1 rule</span><span>&nbsp;and found for the rule &mdash; president </span><span style="font-weight:700">Andreas Mundt</span><span>: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;Die 50+1-Regel kann weiterhin mit dem Ziel der Vereinspr&auml;gung und der Mitgliederpartizipation gerechtfertigt werden.&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;But it found against the way the rule has been applied. Four clubs were given homework: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bayer Leverkusen</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">VfL Wolfsburg</span><span>&nbsp;must reform the benefactor exemptions tied to Bayer AG and Volkswagen; </span><span style="font-weight:700">RB Leipzig</span><span>&nbsp;must give supporters genuine access to ordinary voting membership; and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Hannover 96</span><span>&nbsp;were criticised over the handling of </span><span style="font-weight:700">Martin Kind</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&rsquo;s supervisory-board vote in a 2023/24 secret ballot. The DFL must now equalise membership access across all thirty-six clubs and apply 50+1 consistently in its own internal voting. No deadline has been set; the DFL Pr&auml;sidium will consult the clubs over the coming weeks. This is the most consequential governance ruling in German football in a decade and it went almost entirely unreported outside Germany.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The division has a new look: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Wolfsburg</span><span>&nbsp;were relegated for the first time in their history after twenty-nine consecutive top-flight seasons, along with </span><span style="font-weight:700">St. Pauli</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Heidenheim</span><span>. Up come </span><span style="font-weight:700">SV Elversberg</span><span>, making their Bundesliga debut as the 59th club in the league&rsquo;s history, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Schalke 04</span><span>&nbsp;after three years away, and </span><span style="font-weight:700">SC Paderborn</span><span>. Schalke marked the return in the most Schalke way imaginable: 40,000 retro away shirts, released on 6 August with the historic crest, sold out in two and a half hours. Chief executive </span><span style="font-weight:700">Matthias Tillmann</span><span>: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;You can&rsquo;t plan something like that, it&rsquo;s just crazy&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; and, relaying what an Adidas employee told him, </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">&ldquo;you&rsquo;re the boss of one of the craziest clubs in Europe.&rdquo;</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="font-weight:700">Bayern Munich</span><span>, meanwhile, spent the week lobbying. President </span><span style="font-weight:700">Herbert Hainer</span><span>&nbsp;said flatly that </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;the Bundesliga is clearly, clearly in the background compared to the Premier League&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;and that </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;internationalization cannot be a solo effort by one individual club&rdquo;</span><span>; marketing chief </span><span style="font-weight:700">Rouven Kasper</span><span>&nbsp;noted that only nineteen of thirty-six clubs attend the league&rsquo;s annual meetings. Bayern also look set to keep the architect of their window: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Max Eberl</span><span>&nbsp;is close to an extension beyond summer 2027, with the supervisory board due to decide on 25 August and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Uli Hoene&szlig;</span><span>&nbsp;putting the odds at &ldquo;100 percent&rdquo; while crediting Eberl with &ldquo;a perfect transfer period.&rdquo; The season proper begins with the </span><span style="font-weight:700">Franz Beckenbauer Supercup</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; Bayern against </span><span style="font-weight:700">Borussia Dortmund</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;at Signal Iduna Park on 22 August &mdash; followed by the DFB-Pokal first round, in which Bayern and Dortmund have been held over to 1&ndash;2 September.</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Italy &middot; Serie A</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Serie A opens on </span><span style="font-weight:700">22 August</span><span>, defending a title </span><span style="font-weight:700">Inter</span><span>&nbsp;won for the twenty-first time, but Italian football restarts on Friday with the Coppa Italia round of 32. The managerial map is barely recognisable: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Massimiliano Allegri</span><span>&nbsp;is at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Napoli</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ruben Amorim</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Milan</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Luciano Spalletti</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Juventus</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Maurizio Sarri</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Atalanta</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Gennaro Gattuso</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Lazio</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fabio Grosso</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fiorentina</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Domenico Tedesco</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bologna</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ivan Juri&#263;</span><span>&nbsp;at the promoted </span><span style="font-weight:700">Monza</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ignazio Abate</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Torino</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Cristian Chivu</span><span>&nbsp;holds at Inter and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Gian Piero Gasperini</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Roma</span><span>, where he is already complaining about squad depth: </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">&ldquo;There are teams that can&rsquo;t do business because they&rsquo;re full of players; we have too few.&rdquo;</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Milan are the club with the most audible friction. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Mike Maignan</span><span>&nbsp;is reported unhappy with the club&rsquo;s direction and has delayed his return from holiday, with </span><span style="font-weight:700">Al-Nassr</span><span>&nbsp;circling; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Adrien Rabiot</span><span>&nbsp;also extended his break to the week before kick-off. Amorim has confirmed that squad cuts are coming and is studying a role for Rabiot as a trequartista behind </span><span style="font-weight:700">Gon&ccedil;alo Ramos</span><span>. Napoli, by contrast, are settling: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Giovanni Di Lorenzo</span><span>&nbsp;has agreed an extension to June 2030, described in the Italian press as a </span><span style="font-style:italic">rinnovo a vita</span><span>&nbsp;after 305 appearances, with only the signature outstanding, and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Alex Meret</span><span>&nbsp;said on 12 August the squad is &ldquo;very happy&rdquo; under Allegri. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Lorenzo Pellegrini</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&rsquo;s extension at Roma to 2027 was made official on 9 August.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>Elsewhere: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Atalanta</span><span>&nbsp;lost </span><span style="font-weight:700">Kamaldeen Sulemana</span><span>&nbsp;to a serious knee injury in training on 12 August, out at least two months. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Juventus</span><span>&nbsp;beat </span><span style="font-weight:700">Palermo</span><span>&nbsp;2&ndash;0 in Perth on 11 August through </span><span style="font-weight:700">Kenan Y&#305;ld&#305;z</span><span>&nbsp;on 44 and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Arkadiusz Milik</span><span>&nbsp;on 86. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Daniele Orsato</span><span>, briefing on the 2026/27 refereeing protocol, restated the standard as </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;Var solo in caso di chiaro ed evidente errore.&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;And a story worth chasing rather than printing at length: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Football Italia</span><span>&nbsp;reported on 11 August that </span><span style="font-weight:700">Lazio</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;were forced to terminate a &euro;19m sponsorship with Polymarket after the platform was declared illegal in Italy. That is single-sourced and this paper flags it as such.</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">France &middot; Ligue 1</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>France returns on </span><span style="font-weight:700">Friday 21 August</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Marseille</span><span>&nbsp;against </span><span style="font-weight:700">Strasbourg</span><span>, with every match of the opening round streaming exclusively on the league&rsquo;s own platform, Ligue 1+ &mdash; a wholesale rewiring of French football&rsquo;s economics that passed a million subscribers within a month of launch and remains the subject of an open argument about whether the model works. The division has eighteen clubs: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nantes</span><span>&nbsp;went down after thirteen years up, alongside </span><span style="font-weight:700">Metz</span><span>; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Le Mans</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Troyes</span><span>&nbsp;came up. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nice</span><span>&nbsp;survived the play-off, beating </span><span style="font-weight:700">Saint-&eacute;tienne</span><span>&nbsp;4&ndash;1 on aggregate. </span><span style="font-weight:700">PSG</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;won a record fourteenth title, their fifth in a row, clinching on 13 May.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The story of the French summer is </span><span style="font-weight:700">Olympique Lyonnais</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Michele Kang</span><span>, via Olympe Bidco, acquired 87.78 per cent of the club from Eagle Bidco for $30m in a deal announced on 23 June, subject to regulatory clearance since granted. The transaction eliminated roughly &euro;232.6m of debt, extended creditor repayment deadlines by eighteen months, and commits up to &euro;71m of new capital over two years with &euro;31m injected on completion. </span><span style="font-weight:700">John Textor</span><span>&rsquo;s Eagle Football Group is out; the holding company reverts to the name Olympique Lyonnais Groupe. Kang, who already controls the women&rsquo;s club, called it </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;le plus grand d&eacute;fi de ma vie.&rdquo;</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;The men&rsquo;s and women&rsquo;s clubs will continue to be managed separately.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>The dugouts have turned over almost as hard as England&rsquo;s: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Davide Ancelotti</